    "focal_pair": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 10 USER]\ne219\tOK.\ne220\tOK.\ne221\tI won't call her.\ne222\tTomorrow when I text her the low-stakes thing, what if she doesn't respond.\ne223\tOr what if she responds but with one word.\ne224\tHow do I read that.\n[Turn 10 ASSISTANT]\ne225\tIf she doesn't respond: don't read it as rejection.\ne226\tShe's still in shame, texts take effort, she might not have her phone out, any number of things.\ne227\tSend the text, don't follow up.\ne228\tThe next day, do the same thing — another low-stakes text, no expectation of response.\ne229\tYou're establishing presence, not extracting reply.\ne230\tIf she responds with one word like \"ok\" or \"no\": read that as a yes.\ne231\tShe's engaging.\ne232\tShe's not giving you more than she has to, but she's acknowledging you exist and that the line is open.\ne233\tThat's the beginning of rebuilding.\ne234\tIf she responds with a sentence or a question: don't treat it as the moment.\ne235\tDon't say \"I'm so glad you texted me, can we talk now.\"\ne236\tRespond normally, as if this is how you always text.\ne237\tThe signal you're sending is that she can approach you without a big scene, not that every interaction now carries the weight of everything that happened.\ne238\tThis is going to feel like walking on eggshells for a few weeks.\ne239\tIt is, somewhat.\ne240\tBut it's eggshells on purpose — because the alternative, which is pushing for a big reconciliation moment, almost always backfires with teenagers.",
